  "panel_reasoning_excerpt": "## TEMPLATE 13: XF_NATURE_VIEW_MULTIPATH_001\n**\"Nature exposure \u2192 stress reduction via multiple parallel pathways\"**\n\n**Structural pattern**: stimulus \u2192 MULTIPLE_INDEPENDENT_PATHWAYS \u2192 converging_outcome\n**Higher-order principle**: Multiple mechanism convergence (this template demonstrates Woodward's contributing cause logic \u2014 the effect persists when any single pathway is blocked)\n\nThis is a COMPOSED template linking several base templates:\n\n**Pathway A** (Perceptual efficiency): Nature scene \u2192 PP_SPECTRAL_MATCH_001 \u2192 reduced prediction error \u2192 reduced metabolic demand \u2192 reduced cortisol contribution\n**Pathway B** (Attentional release): Nature scene \u2192 DT_ATTENTIONAL_DEMAND_001 (reversed \u2014 low demand) \u2192 DMN re-engagement \u2192 parasympathetic shift \u2192 reduced cortisol contribution\n**Pathway C** (Affordance relaxation): Nature scene with open prospect \u2192 EC_AFFORDANCE_POSTURAL_001 \u2192 relaxed posture \u2192 vagal tone increase \u2192 reduced cortisol contribution\n**Pathway D** (Allostatic familiarity): Nature scene patterns \u2192 IC_ALLOSTATIC_ANTICIPATION_001 \u2192 improved allostatic predictions \u2192 reduced metabolic cost \u2192 reduced cortisol contribution\n\n**Composition notes**:\n- These pathways are partially independent. A, B, and C operate through different neural systems (visual efficiency, TPN/DMN balance, musculoskeletal-autonomic). D is partially dependent on A (both involve familiarity of natural stimuli).\n- Blocking any single pathway should REDUCE but not ELIMINATE the cortisol effect (contributing causes, per Woodward).\n- The temporal dynamics differ: A is fast (~200ms), B is medium (~minutes), C is medium (~seconds to minutes), D is slow (~hours to days of cumulative exposure).\n\n**Key predictions this template generates**:\n1. Artificial scenes matching Pathway A only (1/f spectra without nature content) should show ~30-40% of the full cortisol effect\n2. Nature images (no physical affordances, blocking Pathway C) should show ~70-80% of the full cortisol effect\n3. The effect should have a complex temporal profile: fast initial drop (A), continued drop over minutes (B+C), and a cumulative benefit with repeated exposure (D)\n4. The effect should be larger than any single-pathway prediction suggests (convergent multiple mechanisms)\n\n**Overall maturity**: **how-plausibly** (individual pathways are at varying maturity; the multi-pathway composition is a how-possibly model that generates testable predictions)\n\n---",
